|~ What is declared under `::keyword`: the refusal of its
   constructor, and the comparisons of keywords, which compare two
   keywords by their names in the one order. ~|

:keyword/KeywordPayloadNotKeywordError
  |~~ `::keyword` reads its payload as the keyword it is; a string
      becomes one by `keyword`.
      ~(
        ::keyword"k"
        !| [type /actualType]
        |  eq [::keyword/KeywordPayloadNotKeywordError ::string]
       )
  ~~|
  ::spec~()

:keyword/gt
  |~~ Whether the subject's name follows the name of the keyword the
      call gives.

      ~(:b | gt :a | eq true)
  ~~|
  ::verb~(:than ::keyword | :returns ::boolean | ::builtin{})

:keyword/gte
  |~~ Whether the subject's name follows or equals the name of the
      keyword the call gives.

      ~(:b | gte :b | eq true)
  ~~|
  ::verb~(:than ::keyword | :returns ::boolean | ::builtin{})

:keyword/lt
  |~~ Whether the subject's name precedes the name of the keyword the
      call gives.

      ~(:a | lt :b | eq true)
  ~~|
  ::verb~(:than ::keyword | :returns ::boolean | ::builtin{})

:keyword/lte
  |~~ Whether the subject's name precedes or equals the name of the
      keyword the call gives.

      ~(:a | lte :a | eq true)
  ~~|
  ::verb~(:than ::keyword | :returns ::boolean | ::builtin{})

:keyword/keyword
  |~~ The name of the keyword, as a string: the reverse of `keyword` on
      a string, so `"foo" | keyword | keyword` answers `"foo"`.

      ~(:foo | keyword | eq "foo")
      ~(:"foo bar" | keyword | eq "foo bar")
  ~~|
  ::verb~(:returns ::string | ::builtin{})

:keyword/runLaws
  |~~ The results of the laws of the page of the binding the keyword
      names in its scope, one `{:law :says :actual :ok :error}` per law,
      `:says` the caption of the claim the law proves. A keyword after
      it runs the laws of the member the binding holds under it.

      ~(:limit |~~ Examples: ~(true) ~~| 10 | :limit | runLaws * /ok | eq [true])
  ~~|
  ::verb~(:anchor #[::keyword ::null] | :returns ::vec | ::builtin{})
